  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 326 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe latest GFS and ECMWF now push the surface front south of the \nforecast area by 12Z Friday, although the mid-upper level trough \naxis is hanging back over western Missouri.  Low level dry air lags \nbehind the surface front according to forecast soundings, and the \nbroad-scale synoptic lift provided by the trough continues to \nproduce light rain along and south of I-70 Friday morning.  Dry air \ndoes eventually filter through the area by Friday afternoon/evening \nas a broad, cool high pressure system builds into the Midwest and \nwestern Great Lakes Region.  The remainder of the forecast through \nTuesday looks seasonably cool and dry.  Daytime temperatures drop \ninto the 60s and low 70s with overnight lows primarily in the 50s. A \nreinforcing shot of cold air drops into the Mississippi Valley as a \nsecond, stronger Canadian high moves into the Midwest Sunday night \nand Monday.  Guidance shows this cold airmass producing some of the \ncoldest overnight lows of the season so far, with temperatures \nbottoming out in the mid to upper 40s both Monday and Tuesday \nmorning.\n\nWill continue to monitor the longer term river flood threat on the \nMissouri and Mississippi Rivers, as well as the tributaries. \nEnsemble hydrologic model output almost universally puts the IQR of \nriver stages above flood at many of our main-stem river points by \nlate this weekend or early next week, mainly due to rainfall well \nupstream.  To be honest, I take this with a large grain of salt as \nonly a small amount of the forecast QPF has fallen at this point. \nThe vast majority of the rainfall that's being forecast and fed into \nthe river models has yet to materialize, and there are many factors \nwhich will affect the levels to which our rivers ultimately\nrise. Still, there is potential for flooding should some of the\nheavier rainfall forecasts be realized.",
